[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Vault module starts from the 42x 42 mm'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gridfinity unit. Extrude it up 42 mm and you get a cube. Triple it in width and length and you get 126x 126x 42 mm, the smallest module in the vault. There are'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'three widths in total. S [music] the smallest at 3x3 units. M the middle at 3x5 and L the [music] largest at 3x 6. Every module also comes in three heights'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '1 2 and four units. Here\'s L at each height. Three heights on M and on S. [music] That gives us nine modules. There are five depth options, too. 3 4 5 6 and seven units.'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Three widths, three heights, five depths. 45 modules in total. The main divider in this system is simply S, M, and L. Depth is the secondary driver. S'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the smallest module in the system. M was designed to make the best possible'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use of a Bamboo Lab P1S build plate. L is double the size of S. And because'

[06:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it was designed by doubling S, the two can be paired and assembled together. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; After 2 months of development, we hit a'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'problem. The modules were bending. Our first instinct was thicker walls, but that cost space, [music] size, and weight. Lightening it with an internal structure instead would only leave us with something that looked like a'

[08:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'regular tool organizer and still wasn\'t strong enough. Looking closer, the bending was happening on the front, the one side with no wall. We added a beam through the middle, but that only worked as a temporary fix. The real cause was simpler. The weight sat right in the'

[08:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'center, kept there so every module could connect to any other. Once we saw that, the fix was obvious. Move the load to the sides. That cost us universal connection. But'

[08:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'honestly, connecting through the center never made much sense anyway, and matching sides could still connect. More importantly, the modules could now stack like bricks, carrying weight'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'straight down instead of pushing against each other in the middle. We dropped the redundant top leg, carved space for the connection, and built the remaining leg straight into the geometry. And since the back wall already carries'

[09:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'its own weight, we only needed that leg up front. Problem, solution, execution. Here\'s how that looks on the vault. One, two, get down.'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] To get the most out of your largest'

[10:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'printer, we also designed an XL model. It comes in a single height printed at a 45° angle to maximize what [music] your printer can produce. [music]'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'To keep things simple, we grouped'

[10:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'modules into families. A family is a width and a depth combined. S7, [music] M5, L3.'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Each family holds three modules, one per height. Pick any family [music] and every module in it connects to the others. S and L are also cross-co compatible [music] at matching depths'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'since L was built from two S\'s. S3 connects to L3. S4 to L4. S7 to L7. [music]'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'One more piece, L2. Same size as L but split down the middle. So you can finish the inside with S-sized facades. At this point, S, L, and L2 modules of'

[11:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any matching depth can all connect together. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[11:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Tell yourself [music] dip.'

[12:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
